WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:15.690
Begin before any print on this wall. At the Heian court, by the twelfth century, the kosode was underwear, worn next to the skin. As court dress grew simpler, the robes above it came off, and the kosode came to the surface.

2
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:32.270
By the early Edo period its shape was not far from today's kimono. Fashion moved fast: a pattern book printed in 1666 records the new Kanbun style, a wide empty back with large, moving motifs.

3
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:42.710
Meiji changed the words. Many garment names collapsed into one: kimono. Becke argues that one shape was frozen in time in these years.

4
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:52.030
Magazines took up the teaching. In one, the tsukesage had nearly vanished by the two-thousands, folded into the visiting kimono.

5
00:00:52.350 --> 00:01:10.490
In 1981 the kimono retail market was worth nearly one point eight trillion yen. By 2020 it was about a seventh of that. Kimono became something rented rather than owned; by 2021 Kyoto alone had about forty rental shops.

6
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.790
By then the shape had long since travelled. The wall goes on, abroad.
